        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 160,
        "headingText": "<p>9040/3 Synovial sarcoma NOS</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-11-22T17:42:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 361,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 161,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56164,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 161,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B5A.Y &amp; XH9B22 Synovial sarcoma, other specified primary site &amp; Synovial sarcoma NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 362,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56165,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 162,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 363,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56166,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 163,
        "headingText": "<p>Synovial sarcoma, monophasic fibrous (9041/3); synovial sarcoma, biphasic (9043/3)</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-11-22T17:42:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56167,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Primary synovial sarcoma is very rare in the GI tract and occurs primarily in the stomach, but a small number of cases have been reported in the oesophagus, small intestine, and colon.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56168,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the GI tract, the reported occurrence is preferentially in middle-aged or older adults, with no sex predilection, although examples have been reported in children, especially in the oesophagus. Synovial sarcoma may occur as a polypoid or ulcerated mucosal lesion or a transmural mass lesion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56169,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56170,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56171,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gene rearrangements are common and can be helpful for diagnosis (see &lt;&gt;<em>Diagnostic molecular pathology</em>, below).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56172,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common presentation in the oesophagus is a polypoid mucosal mass. In the stomach, cases with early diagnosis form a cup-like or plaque-like mucosal lesion or a subserosal mass of 1&ndash;2&nbsp;cm. Mucosal tumours usually extend into the submucosa and often also involve the muscularis propria. More-advanced tumours form transmural masses of 5&nbsp;cm to &gt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;cm.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56173,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "<p>Most gastrointestinal synovial sarcomas are monophasic spindle cell tumours composed of relatively uniform spindle cells with almost no matrix or with a focal collagenous matrix. A minority of cases have a biphasic histology, with glandular elements and spindle cells, similar to the monophasic tumours. Mitotic counts vary, but most cases have a low-grade appearance with scarce mitoses. High-grade components with a round cell pattern and prominent mitotic activity may be present and portend a poorer prognosis. Immunohistochemically, the lesion shows patchy, focal positivity for keratin and EMA in the monophasic spindle cell tumours and strong positivity in the glandular elements. Positivity for calretinin, S100, DOG1, and rarely KIT (KIT usually being focal) can occur, and this raises the differential diagnostic consideration of gastrointestinal stromal tumour.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-10-15T17:33:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56174,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 170,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 371,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 171,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56175,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 171,
        "headingText": "<p>Like other synovial sarcomas, those in the GI tract feature <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX1</em> or <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX2</em> gene fusions (rarely, <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX4</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 374,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56176,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 174,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> spindle cells, sometimes with a biphasic epithelioid or glandular component; patchy expression of keratins and/or EMA in spindle cells; <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX1/2/4</em> rearrangement.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-05-03T10:10:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 372,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 172,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56177,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 172,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This neoplasm is not formally staged according to the TNM soft tissue tumours classification system.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 373,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 173,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56178,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 215,
        "headingId": 173,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The available follow-up data suggest that small mucosal synovial sarcomas with low-grade features have an excellent prognosis after complete excision, whereas smaller tumours with high-grade components and larger transmural tumours are associated with metastatic behaviour.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:39:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]